 <section anchor="verification-protocol"><name>Verification Protocol</name>
-<t>A relying party verifying a receipt MUST execute the following sequence and treat any failure as a malformed receipt with gate decision <tt>halt</tt>:</t>
+<t>A relying party verifying a receipt MUST execute the following sequence. Except where a step states otherwise (a content-hash mismatch in the evidence-set step, <xref target="evidence-set-resolution"/>, resolves <tt>unknown</tt> and does not halt), any check that runs and fails makes the receipt malformed, with gate decision <tt>halt</tt>. Step 1's unresolved-key and refusal outcomes (<xref target="key-resolution"/>) also halt, but are not malformed and are not failed checks:</t>
 
-<sourcecode type="text"><![CDATA[1. Verify JWS signature against issuer's published JWKS
-   (RFC 7515).
+<sourcecode type="text"><![CDATA[1. Resolve kid in the trust material the relying party
+   supplied (Section 5.4.2). If a key resolves, verify the
+   JWS signature with it (RFC 7515): the step reports
+   signature_verified, or signature_invalid, which is
+   malformed; gate decision = halt. If no key resolves, the
+   step reports key_unresolved and yields no check result;
+   if the relying party declared its trust material
+   complete, the receipt is also refused under that policy.
+   Either way gate decision = halt and evaluation stops.
 2. Resolve v_gate_mapping -> fetch the named immutable
